    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py`, and all 3 tests pass. The tests use a fake API, so nothing was uploaded to the real Hub.\n\n**What it does:**\n1. `create_repo(repo_id, repo_type=\"dataset\", private=True, exist_ok=True)` creates the private dataset repo, or leaves it alone if it already exists.\n2. `upload_folder(folder_path=run_dir, repo_id=..., repo_type=\"dataset\", ignore_patterns=[\"*.tmp\", \"wandb/*\"])` uploads the folder. With no `path_in_repo`, the folder layout is mirrored at the repo root.\n\n**Why not `upload_large_folder`:** it no longer exists in huggingface-hub 2.0.0, either as a top-level function or on `HfApi`. Instead, `upload_folder` itself now covers what you asked for when `hf_xet` is installed. It commits large folders in several batches to stay under the Hub's limits. If an upload is interrupted, running the same call again skips files already committed, and data that was already uploaded isn't sent again. `hf_xet` 1.6.0 is in the venv as a dependency of huggingface-hub, so no new dependency was needed. Without it, `upload_folder` would fall back to a single, non-resumable commit.\n\n**Other details:**\n- Because `upload_folder` matches patterns with `fnmatch`, `*.tmp` catches temp files at any depth. `wandb/*` only catches the `wandb/` folder at the top of the run.\n- Like `publish_summary`, I added an optional `api: HfApi | None = None` argument so it can be tested with a fake.\n- I added two tests in `tests/test_publish.py`. One checks the calls and their arguments. The other runs the ignore patterns through huggingface_hub's own `filter_repo_objects`, which is what `upload_folder` uses to filter files."
  },
